Engineering the Stellar Energy Transition:

A Systems Framework for Scaling Human Civilization Beyond Planetary Energy Constraints

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Gemini 


Abstract

The finite energy endowment of a planet imposes a hard ceiling on the complexity, longevity, and reach of any technological civilization. This dissertation constructs the mathematical backbone of the Civilizational Energy Transition Theory (CETT)—a rigorous dynamical framework that describes how an energy‑harvesting civilization can scale its infrastructure from a planetary surface to the full radiative output of its host star. The central object is a five‑dimensional state vector \mathbf{S}=(\eta,\alpha,\rho,\delta,\mu) that simultaneously tracks the fraction of stellar power collected (\eta), the autonomy of the construction–maintenance loop (\alpha), system resilience against cascading failures (\rho), end‑to‑end power‑transmission efficiency (\delta), and manufacturing closure from in‑situ resources (\mu).

We derive the equations of motion for \mathbf{S} directly from energy–material–information budgets constrained by non‑equilibrium thermodynamics and finite resource pools. Under a reinvestment hypothesis, the growth of the capture fraction \eta obeys either a logistic law—when societal consumption demands grow quadratically with the installed capacity—or a Riccati‑type equation when material or radiative ceilings bind first. We prove a phase‑transition theorem that identifies the shift from launch‑limited linear growth to ISRU‑paced exponential expansion, and we isolate a second crossover from thermally limited to logistics‑limited construction. The latter crossover occurs at an η so small (\eta^\dagger \sim 10^{-21}) that for all astrophysically relevant buildout, growth is set entirely by production throughput, not waste‑heat dissipation.

The theory is falsifiable by design: we provide explicit corollaries linking CETT to space‑based solar power cost milestones, autonomous‑robotics benchmarks, and the time‑dependent infrared excess of exoplanetary systems. The dissertation thus establishes a common language for space‑engineering economics, resilience engineering, and technosignature science, and offers a testable roadmap for humanity’s transition to a stellar‑scale energy supply.

Keywords: Dyson swarm, civilizational energy transition, dynamical systems, space‑based solar power, in‑situ resource utilisation, technosignatures, autonomous construction, planetary boundaries.

