Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Planetary Energy Bottleneck

Every technological civilization is, at its most fundamental level, a dissipative structure that maintains its internal complexity by extracting high‑grade energy from its environment and rejecting waste heat. On Earth, the accessible energy flux is bounded by the solar constant (∼1361 W m⁻²), the planet’s cross‑sectional area, and the thermodynamic efficiency with which that flux can be converted into useful work. Even with a fully renewable global energy system, the ultimate cap on terrestrial power production is set by the Earth’s radiative balance: exceeding roughly 10–20 TW of waste heat would trigger unacceptable climate disruption (Kleidon, 2016). Current human civilization consumes approximately 18 TW of primary energy; a growth‑oriented trajectory would inevitably collide with this planetary boundary within a few centuries.

The way out of this trap is to move the energy‑harvesting apparatus off the planet and into space, where the full luminosity of the Sun—3.828\times10^{26} W—is available, unattenuated by atmosphere and unconstrained by Earth’s fragile heat budget. The construction of a Sun‑orbiting swarm of solar collectors, often called a Dyson swarm, offers a factor of roughly 2\times10^{10} increase in accessible power. Realizing even a tiny fraction of that potential would permanently decouple civilization’s material ambitions from planetary limits and open the door to interstellar expansion.

Yet moving from a planetary to a stellar energy base is not merely an engineering challenge; it is a civilizational phase transition governed by a set of tightly coupled physical, economic, and institutional constraints. Capturing even 0.1 % of the solar output requires a swarm of collectors whose total mass, depending on specific mass design, could be comparable to the entire asteroid belt, and whose construction must be sustained by an autonomous manufacturing chain that extracts materials from space, fabricates components, and maintains the fleet against attrition—all without a continuous umbilical to Earth. The transition is thus a problem in nonlinear dynamics, constrained optimization, and resilience engineering.

Despite a rich literature on Dyson sphere searches (Dyson, 1960; Wright et al., 2014; Zackrisson et al., 2015), space‑based solar power economics (Mankins, 2012; Potter et al., 2023), and in‑situ resource utilization (Metzger et al., 2013), there exists no unified mathematical framework that ties these threads together and allows one to ask: under what conditions does a civilization complete this transition, what are the speed limits, and can we detect such a transition in another star system?

This dissertation supplies that framework.

1.2 The Civilizational Energy Transition Theory (CETT)

We define the Civilizational Energy Transition Theory as a dynamical systems model of the growth of a stellar‑scale energy collection infrastructure. CETT posits that the macrostate of the system can be captured by a small number of dimensionless aggregate variables—capture fraction, autonomy, resilience, distribution efficiency, and manufacturing closure—and that the evolution of these variables is governed by the reinvestment of a portion of the captured power back into the manufacturing chain that builds and maintains the collectors.

The theory does not design a particular swarm architecture (Dyson ring, bubble, statite, etc.) but instead treats the infrastructure as an abstract energy‑conversion stock whose growth follows from a global energy budget. This abstraction is deliberate: it allows the model to be calibrated against real‑world data—the levelized cost of space‑based solar power, launch rates, automation benchmarks—and to make predictions that are independent of detailed engineering choices.

CETT is falsifiable. If the real‑world cost of space‑based solar power does not fall below a critical threshold by a certain date, or if robotic assembly fails to reach a required autonomy level, the theory’s optimistic growth trajectories are ruled out. Conversely, if an exoplanetary system shows an infrared excess whose time evolution fits the logistic or Riccati forms derived here, CETT would be supported as a universal template for the energy trajectories of technological species.

1.3 Research Questions and Objectives

The dissertation is organized around four overarching questions:

1. Dynamics: What are the minimal equations of motion that describe the growth of a stellar‑energy‑harvesting infrastructure, and what are the possible saturation forms (logistic, Riccati, generalized logistic) that arise from material, thermal, and societal constraints?
2. Limits: What are the absolute physical ceilings on the capture fraction η and on the growth rate \dot\eta, as imposed by thermodynamics, material inventories, and the finite speed of construction?
3. Phase Transitions: Under what conditions does the system switch from externally‑paced growth (launch‑limited) to autonomous exponential growth (ISRU‑limited), and from thermally‑limited to logistics‑limited construction? What are the observable signatures of these transitions?
4. Falsifiability and Technosignatures: Can CETT generate testable predictions that link near‑term space‑engineering milestones to the long‑term detectability of stellar‑scale infrastructure around other stars?

The primary objective is to provide a rigorous, mathematical answer to these questions, in the form of a set of theorems, lemmas, and corollaries that form a coherent theoretical edifice. A secondary objective is to calibrate the model against existing and near‑future data, and to perform sensitivity analyses that identify the most critical engineering levers for accelerating the transition.

1.4 Scope and Delimitations

This work is a theoretical study. It does not design or cost a specific swarm architecture; rather, it provides the generic dynamical scaffolding within which any such architecture must operate. Multi‑star systems, interstellar energy distribution, and the detailed physics of power beaming are deferred to future extensions. The model assumes a single host star and a civilization that makes rational, energy‑budget‑constrained decisions about reinvestment; strategic or game‑theoretic extensions are noted but not pursued in depth.




Chapter 2: Mathematical Framework

2.1 Primitive Concepts and the State Space

A civilizational energy system at time t is a tuple \mathcal{C}(t) = (\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{G}) where \mathcal{I} is the set of active energy‑collection infrastructure elements, \mathcal{R} the set of extractable material resources, \mathcal{T} the technology suite, and \mathcal{G} the governance/institutional layer. To construct a tractable dynamical model, we coarse‑grain this complexity into a five‑dimensional macrostate.

Definition 2.1 (State vector).
The macrostate is given by the dimensionless vector

\mathbf{S}(t) = \bigl(\eta(t), \alpha(t), \rho(t), \delta(t), \mu(t)\bigr) \in [0,1]^5,
\]  

where

\begin{aligned}
\eta(t) &\equiv \frac{P_{\text{captured}}(t)}{L_\ast}, \qquad \text{\(L_\ast\) stellar luminosity (W)},\\[4pt]
\alpha(t) &\equiv \frac{\text{automated construction‑maintenance throughput}}{\text{total required throughput}},\\[4pt]
\rho(t) &\equiv \mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac{\text{capacity post‑failure}}{\text{nominal capacity}}\right] \text{under a defined stochastic disruption model},\\[4pt]
\delta(t) &\equiv \frac{P_{\text{delivered}}(t)}{P_{\text{captured}}(t)}, \qquad \text{end‑to‑end transmission‑conversion efficiency},\\[4pt]
\mu(t) &\equiv \frac{\text{mass of infrastructure fabricable from ISRU}}{\text{total mass of infrastructure}} .
\end{aligned}

All quantities are defined with respect to a single host star. Multi‑star generalisations are deferred.

Axiom 2.2 (Locality of constraints).
Each component of \mathbf{S} is bounded above by physical limits that depend only on known physics and the material inventory of the host system. In particular, \eta \le \eta_{\max}(\text{materials, thermodynamics}) < 1 for any swarm or shell architecture.

2.2 Dynamical Equations of the State Vector

The time evolution of the infrastructure stock C = \eta L_\ast (measured in Watts of captured power) is governed by the balance of construction, maintenance, and degradation:

\frac{dC}{dt} = \Pi(C, \mathbf{R}, \mathbf{A}) - \lambda_d C, \tag{1}

where \Pi is the effective construction power (W of new collection capacity produced per unit time), \mathbf{R} is the accessible resource inventory, \mathbf{A} is the automation vector, and \lambda_d is the exponential decay rate due to attrition and finite lifetimes.

Assumption 2.3 (Reinvestment coupling).
A fraction \epsilon(t) \in [0,1] of the delivered power P_{\text{delivered}} = \eta L_\ast \delta is reinvested into the industrial system that builds and maintains the collectors. The remaining power sustains the civilization’s consumption and governance.

Under this hypothesis, the construction power is bounded by the available manufacturing energy:

\Pi \le \epsilon \, \eta \, L_\ast \, \delta \, \alpha \, \mu \, \zeta, \tag{2}

where \zeta (W of collector capacity produced per Joule of manufacturing energy) is the specific construction efficiency.

Proposition 2.4 (Growth equation for \eta).
If the construction power saturates the available reinvested energy and infrastructure decays at rate \lambda_d, then

\frac{d\eta}{dt} = \epsilon \zeta \delta \alpha \mu \, \eta - \lambda_d \eta . \tag{3}

Proof. From C = \eta L_\ast and (1), \dot{\eta} L_\ast = \Pi - \lambda_d \eta L_\ast. Substituting the saturated form of (2) and dividing by L_\ast yields (3). ∎

Equation (3) is linear in \eta if the prefactors are constant, leading to unbounded exponential growth. In reality, these parameters co‑evolve with \eta and saturate growth.

Definition 2.5 (Extended state equations).
The full coupled system is:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{d\eta}{dt} &= \bigl( \epsilon(\mathbf{S},t)\,\zeta(\mu,\alpha)\,\delta\,\alpha\,\mu - \lambda_d \bigr) \eta, \tag{4a}\\[4pt]
\frac{d\alpha}{dt} &= g_\alpha(\eta,\alpha, \text{AI‑robotics investment}), \tag{4b}\\[4pt]
\frac{d\delta}{dt} &= g_\delta(\text{beaming technology, network size}), \tag{4c}\\[4pt]
\frac{d\mu}{dt} &= g_\mu(\text{ISRU capability, resource exhaustion}), \tag{4d}\\[4pt]
\frac{d\rho}{dt} &= g_\rho(\text{redundancy investment, failure data}). \tag{4e}
\end{aligned}

The functions g_\alpha, g_\delta, g_\mu, g_\rho will be specified in later chapters.

2.3 Saturation Forms and Growth Laws

We now derive explicit forms for \eta(t) by closing the reinvestment ratio \epsilon and the specific construction efficiency \zeta.

Lemma 2.6 (Resource‑limited specific efficiency).
Let the most accessible material reserves be exhausted as \eta grows, with \zeta(\eta) = \zeta_0 \left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right)^\nu (\nu>0). Then \zeta \to 0 as \eta \to \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}.

Proof. See Appendix A. ∎

Lemma 2.7 (Radiative‑thermal bound on \eta).
A swarm intercepting a fraction \eta of stellar output must re‑radiate the unconverted fraction. The equilibrium temperature of the collectors cannot exceed the material melting point, imposing

\eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}} = \left(\frac{T_{\max}}{T_{\text{eff},\ast}}\right)^4 \cdot \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}}.

For the Sun and A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}}=1, \eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}} \approx 0.02–0.05 for refractory‑metal radiators near 2200–2800 K. (Full derivation in Appendix B.)

Theorem 2.8 (Logistic saturation from societal budget constraint).
If delivered power is partitioned as \eta L_\ast \delta = C_{\text{soc}} + I with C_{\text{soc}} = c_0 \eta^\theta and I = k \dot{\eta}, then for \theta=2 we obtain the logistic equation

\dot\eta = r_0 \eta\left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}}\right),\quad r_0 = \frac{L_\ast\delta}{k},\; \eta_{\max} = \frac{L_\ast\delta}{c_0}.

Proof. See Proposition 3.5 in the main text; solution yields the familiar S‑curve.

Corollary 2.9 (Physical ceilings → Riccati‑type growth).
If the material ceiling binds first, inserting \zeta(\eta)=\zeta_0(1-\eta/\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}) into (4a) yields a Riccati equation

\dot\eta = \eta\left[ \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu\left(1-\frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right) - \lambda_d \right],

which is not logistic; at the special point where \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu = \lambda_d it reduces to \dot\eta = -(\lambda_d/\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}})\eta^2. Whether the system evolves logistically or under a Riccati law is an empirical discriminant (§9).

2.4 Uniqueness and Stability of the Fixed Point

Theorem 2.10 (Uniqueness and stability of the nontrivial fixed point).
Assume the institutional control rule \epsilon(\mathbf{S},t) is a non‑increasing function of \eta (a natural reading of reinvestment satiation). Let the net growth rate be r(\eta) \equiv \epsilon(\eta)\zeta(\eta)\delta\alpha\mu - \lambda_d, and suppose r(0^+) > 0 while r(\eta_{\max}) < 0 with \eta_{\max} = \min\{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}, \eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}}\}. Then there exists a unique \eta^* \in (0,\eta_{\max}) solving r(\eta^*)=0. Coupled with the monotonic saturation of the other state variables, the full system possesses a unique fixed point \mathbf{S}^*. If the Jacobian at \mathbf{S}^* has all eigenvalues with negative real part, \mathbf{S}^* is locally asymptotically stable.

Proof. (Detailed proof as in the revised theorem, using monotonicity and Gershgorin circle theorem; see Appendix I.)

Remark. If institutions increase reinvestment with \eta (escalation of commitment), r(\eta) can become non‑monotone, opening the possibility of multiple equilibria and bistability—a civilization trapped at a low‑\eta state unless pushed past a threshold.

2.5 Thermodynamic Constraints and the Two‑Regime Growth Limit

Definition 2.11 (Waste‑heat flux).
Q_{\text{waste}} = (1 - \delta)\eta L_\ast + \delta\eta L_\ast (1 - \varepsilon_{\text{use}}), with \varepsilon_{\text{use}} the end‑use efficiency.

Theorem 2.12 (Second‑law bound – two‑regime form).
Define the thermal‑dissipation‑limited and build‑time‑limited construction rates:

\dot N_{\text{therm}}(\eta) = \frac{A_{\text{rad,0}}\,\eta\,\sigma T_{\max}^4}{E_{\text{fab}}\,\eta_{\text{diss}}},\qquad \dot N_{\text{build}} = \frac{1}{\tau_{\text{build}}}.

The actual maximum construction rate is \dot N_{\max}(\eta) = \min\{\dot N_{\text{therm}}, \dot N_{\text{build}}\}, giving

\dot\eta_{\max}(\eta) = \frac{P_0}{L_\ast}\min\!\left\{\frac{A_{\text{rad,0}}\,\eta\,\sigma T_{\max}^4}{E_{\text{fab}}\,\eta_{\text{diss}}},\; \frac{1}{\tau_{\text{build}}}\right\}.

The branches cross at \eta^\dagger = \frac{E_{\text{fab}}\,\eta_{\text{diss}}}{\sigma T_{\max}^4\,A_{\text{rad,0}}\,\tau_{\text{build}}}. For \eta < \eta^\dagger, growth is thermally limited; for \eta > \eta^\dagger, it is logistics‑limited.

Proof. (Full derivation in Appendix C; an elegant mass‑flow form k_{\text{therm}} = a_{\text{rad,spec}}\sigma T_{\max}^4/(e_{\text{fab}}\eta_{\text{diss}}) is also presented.)

Corollary 2.13 (Numeric irrelevance of the thermal branch).
Under baseline aerospace assumptions (T_{\max}=2500 K, a_{\text{rad,spec}}=1 m²/kg, e_{\text{fab}}=10^8 J/kg), k_{\text{therm}} \approx 0.022 s⁻¹, while the logistics branch gives k_{\text{build}} \sim 10^{-23} s⁻¹. Thus \eta^\dagger \approx 2\times10^{-21}, which is many orders of magnitude below the launch‑to‑ISRU transition \eta_c\sim 10^{-9}–10^{-7}. Consequently, for all astrophysically relevant buildout, growth is logistics‑limited, never thermally limited. The static ceiling \eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}} remains the ultimate cap, but the rate of approach is determined by production throughput.

This completes the core mathematical framework. In the chapters that follow, we specify the dynamics of \alpha, \delta, \mu, \rho, analyze the phase transitions in detail, and translate the theory into falsifiable predictions.



Chapter 3

Saturation Forms and Growth Laws

The bare growth equation (4a) admits unbounded exponential increase if the prefactors \epsilon,\zeta,\delta,\alpha,\mu are held constant. In reality, each of these parameters evolves with \eta—because the best ores are exhausted first, the societal demand for energy rises, and the reinvestment fraction is itself a decision variable subject to institutional constraints. This chapter derives the explicit functional forms of the saturation from micro‑economic and physical principles, leading to logistic, Riccati, or generalised logistic growth. We also prove that under mild monotonicity conditions the system possesses a unique, locally stable nontrivial fixed point.

---

3.1 Reinvestment, Consumption, and the Logistic Law

We begin with a macro‑economic budget that determines the reinvestment fraction \epsilon endogenously.

Proposition 3.1 (Microfoundation for logistic and generalised logistic laws).
Assume the total delivered power \eta L_\ast \delta is partitioned between societal consumption C_{\text{soc}} and reinvestment I that directly builds new capacity. Further assume that consumption demand grows with the size of the installed base as C_{\text{soc}} = c_0 \eta^\theta with c_0>0,\ \theta>0, and that the manufacturing sector requires an investment I = k \dot{\eta} (energy per unit time proportional to the rate of change of capture fraction). Then the energy balance

\eta L_\ast \delta = c_0 \eta^\theta + k \dot{\eta}

yields

\dot{\eta} = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{k} \eta - \frac{c_0}{k} \eta^\theta . \tag{3.1}

Proof. Solve the balance for \dot\eta. ∎

The special case \theta = 2 gives the standard logistic equation:

\dot\eta = r_0 \eta\left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}}\right), \qquad
r_0 = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{k},\quad \eta_{\max} = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{c_0}. \tag{3.2}

The solution is the familiar S‑curve

\eta(t) = \frac{\eta_{\max}}{1 + \left(\frac{\eta_{\max}}{\eta_0}-1\right)e^{-r_0 t}}. \tag{3.3}

If \theta>1 but \theta\neq 2, (3.1) is a generalised logistic equation; its solution can be expressed in terms of the incomplete beta function. The exponent \theta captures how strongly society’s energy appetite grows with the scale of the system—a value \theta=2 corresponds to the plausible scenario in which the demand scales with the square of the collection area (e.g., because the civilisation expands into a two‑dimensional network of habitats).

---

3.2 Resource‑Limited Construction Efficiency

The specific construction efficiency \zeta (W of collector capacity produced per joule of manufacturing energy) cannot remain constant indefinitely: the richest and most accessible ores are consumed first, and the energy cost of extracting and refining subsequent material increases.

Lemma 3.2 (Resource‑limited specific efficiency).
Let \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}} denote the maximum capture fraction allowed by the total useful structural material in the system. If the manufacturing energy per kilogram rises as the grade of accessible ore declines, the efficiency can be modeled as

\zeta(\eta) = \zeta_0 \left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right)^\nu, \qquad \nu>0, \tag{3.4}

so that \zeta(\eta) \to 0 as \eta \to \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}.

Proof. The total collector mass is M = m_{\text{specific}} \eta L_\ast / \mathcal{F}, with m_{\text{specific}} the mass per unit collected power and \mathcal{F} the local solar flux. Equating M \le M_{\text{tot}} (the accessible material inventory) gives \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}} = M_{\text{tot}} \mathcal{F} / (m_{\text{specific}} L_\ast). As \eta approaches this limit, ever leaner ores must be processed, and the specific energy e_{\text{fab}} (J kg⁻¹) increases. Because \zeta \propto 1/e_{\text{fab}}, a simple power‑law depletion yields (3.4). The exponent \nu depends on the ore‑grade distribution; for uniform grade it is 1. ∎

---

3.3 Radiative‑Thermal Ceiling on \eta

Lemma 3.3 (Radiative‑thermal bound).
A swarm of collectors intercepting a fraction \eta of the stellar output must re‑radiate the unconverted fraction. If each collector’s radiating area is comparable to its collecting area, the equilibrium temperature cannot exceed the material’s maximum operating temperature T_{\max}. This imposes an absolute cap

\eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}} = \left(\frac{T_{\max}}{T_{\text{eff},\ast}}\right)^4 \cdot \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}}, \tag{3.5}

where T_{\text{eff},\ast} is the star’s effective temperature and A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}} is the ratio of radiating to collecting areas.

Proof sketch. In the worst‑case limit (collectors placed arbitrarily close to the star), the absorbed flux is \sigma T_{\text{eff},\ast}^4 and the emitted flux is \sigma T_{\max}^4 per unit radiator area. Setting them equal under the assumption that all intercepted power must be radiated yields (3.5). A full derivation with a discussion of distance‑dependent loosening is given in Appendix B. ∎

For a Sun‑like star (T_{\text{eff}} = 5778 K) and A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}}=1, the ceiling for common materials is:

T_{\max} (K) Material regime \eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}}
1200 Silicon/aluminum 0.0019
1500 High‑temp alloys 0.0045
2000 Advanced ceramics 0.0145
2500 Refractory metals 0.0354
3000 Near sublimation limits 0.0733

Thus reaching \eta \sim 0.02–0.05 requires refractory‑metal radiators operating near 2200–2800 K—physically defensible but aggressive.

---

3.4 Riccati‑Type Growth When the Material Ceiling Binds First

Inserting the depletion law (3.4) with \nu=1 into the growth equation (4a) (with all other parameters temporarily constant) yields

\dot\eta = \eta\left[ \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu\left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right) - \lambda_d \right]. \tag{3.6}

This is a Riccati equation; it is not a logistic. It can be rewritten as \dot\eta = a\eta - b\eta^2 with a = \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu - \lambda_d and b = \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu / \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}. The carrying capacity (when a>0) is \eta^* = a/b = \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}(1 - \lambda_d/(\epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu)). If the net growth coefficient a is positive, the system approaches \eta^* logistically with a modified ceiling; if a=0 (a precise balance between production and decay at the beginning), the equation reduces to \dot\eta = -b\eta^2, i.e., pure quadratic decay—a physically fleeting condition but mathematically informative.

Crucially, whether the real system follows a logistic (economic‑ceiling driven) or a Riccati (material‑ceiling driven) trajectory is an empirical question, and it provides a falsifiable discriminant for the theory (see Chapter 8).

---

3.5 Generalized Logistic Forms

To encompass a wider range of saturation behaviours without committing to a particular micro‑foundation, we may write phenomenologically

\frac{1}{\eta}\frac{d\eta}{dt} = r_0 \left[1 - \left(\frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}}\right)^\phi \right]^\psi, \qquad \phi,\psi>0. \tag{3.7}

The logistic corresponds to \phi=1,\psi=1; the Gompertz law emerges in the limit \psi\to0^+ with \phi=1 after a suitable rescaling. The parameters can be estimated from historical energy‑growth data or from simulations that include explicit resource and social dynamics.

