Appendix A

Proofs of the Growth Equations

A.1 Proposition 2.2 – Growth Equation for \eta

Statement. Let the total construction power \Pi saturate the available reinvested energy, and let the infrastructure decay rate be \lambda_d. Then the capture fraction obeys

\frac{d\eta}{dt} = \epsilon \zeta \delta \alpha \mu \, \eta - \lambda_d \eta . \tag{3}

Proof. By definition, the installed collection capacity is C = \eta L_\ast. The time evolution of capacity is given by (1):

\frac{dC}{dt} = \Pi - \lambda_d C .

Substituting C = \eta L_\ast gives

L_\ast \frac{d\eta}{dt} = \Pi - \lambda_d \eta L_\ast .

The saturated construction power from (2) is \Pi = \epsilon \, \eta \, L_\ast \, \delta \, \alpha \, \mu \, \zeta. Inserting this into the balance yields

L_\ast \frac{d\eta}{dt} = \epsilon \eta L_\ast \delta \alpha \mu \zeta - \lambda_d \eta L_\ast .

Dividing both sides by L_\ast (non‑zero) gives exactly (3). ∎

A.2 Proposition 3.5 – Logistic and Generalised Logistic from a Budget Constraint

Statement. Assume P_{\text{delivered}} = \eta L_\ast \delta is partitioned as C_{\text{soc}} + I with C_{\text{soc}} = c_0 \eta^\theta and I = k \dot{\eta}. Then

\dot{\eta} = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{k} \eta - \frac{c_0}{k} \eta^\theta . \tag{P3.5}

Proof. The budget constraint is

\eta L_\ast \delta = c_0 \eta^\theta + k \dot{\eta}.

Solve for \dot{\eta}:

k \dot{\eta} = \eta L_\ast \delta - c_0 \eta^\theta,

\dot{\eta} = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{k} \eta - \frac{c_0}{k} \eta^\theta .

∎

Special case \theta = 2 (logistic):

\dot{\eta} = \frac{L_\ast \delta}{k} \eta - \frac{c_0}{k} \eta^2 = r_0 \eta \left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}}\right),

with r_0 = L_\ast \delta / k and \eta_{\max} = L_\ast \delta / c_0. The solution is obtained by separation of variables:

\frac{d\eta}{\eta(1-\eta/\eta_{\max})} = r_0 \, dt,

\int \left( \frac{1}{\eta} + \frac{1/\eta_{\max}}{1 - \eta/\eta_{\max}} \right) d\eta = r_0 t + C,

\ln \eta - \ln(1 - \eta/\eta_{\max}) = r_0 t + C,

\frac{\eta}{1 - \eta/\eta_{\max}} = C' e^{r_0 t}.

At t=0, \eta=\eta_0 yields C' = \eta_0/(1 - \eta_0/\eta_{\max}). Solving for \eta gives the logistic function:

\eta(t) = \frac{\eta_{\max}}{1 + \left(\frac{\eta_{\max}}{\eta_0} - 1\right) e^{-r_0 t}} .

A.3 Corollary 3.3′ – Riccati‑type Equation Under Material Ceiling

Statement. If the material ceiling \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}} binds first, Lemma 3.1 forces \zeta(\eta)=\zeta_0(1-\eta/\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}). With all other parameters constant, the growth equation becomes

\dot{\eta} = \eta\left[ \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu\left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right) - \lambda_d \right],

which is a Riccati equation.

Proof. Substitute \zeta(\eta) = \zeta_0(1 - \eta/\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}) into (4a):

\dot{\eta} = \epsilon \zeta_0 \left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right) \delta \alpha \mu \, \eta - \lambda_d \eta .

Factor \eta:

\dot{\eta} = \eta \left[ \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu - \lambda_d - \frac{\epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}} \, \eta \right].

This has the form \dot{\eta} = a\eta - b\eta^2 with a = \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu - \lambda_d and b = \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu / \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}, i.e. a Riccati equation. At the special point a = 0 (when \epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu = \lambda_d), it reduces to \dot{\eta} = -b\eta^2 = -(\lambda_d/\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}})\eta^2. ∎

The solution for a \neq 0 can be obtained by separation of variables, leading to a logistic function with carrying capacity \eta^* = a/b = \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}(1 - \lambda_d/(\epsilon\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu)), provided a>0. If a<0, the only non‑negative fixed point is \eta=0, and the capacity decays.

A.4 Generalised Logistic Forms (Assumption 3.4)

The phenomenological equation

\frac{1}{\eta}\frac{d\eta}{dt} = r_0 \left[1 - \left(\frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}}\right)^\phi \right]^\psi

contains the logistic (\phi=1,\psi=1) and Gompertz (limit \psi \to 0_+ with \phi=1 after suitable rescaling) as special cases. Its integration is possible by quadrature but does not yield elementary functions in general.

