Appendix B

Thermodynamic and Material Ceilings

B.1 Full Derivation of \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}} (Lemma 3.1)

Definitions.
Let m_{\text{specific}} be the mass of collector required per unit of captured power (kg/W). The total mass of collectors deployed for captured power P_{\text{capt}} = \eta L_\ast is

M = m_{\text{specific}} \cdot \eta L_\ast .

Material inventory constraint. Let the total accessible mass of useful structural material in the system be M_{\text{tot}}. Then the requirement M \le M_{\text{tot}} yields

m_{\text{specific}} \, \eta L_\ast \le M_{\text{tot}} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \eta \le \frac{M_{\text{tot}}}{m_{\text{specific}} L_\ast}.

Thus we define

\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}} = \frac{M_{\text{tot}}}{m_{\text{specific}} L_\ast}.

Depletion model for \zeta. Assume that manufacturing energy per kilogram increases as richer ores are exhausted. A simple phenomenological form is

\zeta(\eta) = \zeta_0 \left(1 - \frac{\eta}{\eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}}\right)^\nu, \qquad \nu>0.

When \eta \to \eta_{\max}^{\text{mat}}, the available material is fully committed, and no further capacity can be built with the same energy investment; mathematically, \zeta \to 0. This completes the proof. ∎

B.2 Radiative‑Thermal Ceiling – Full Calculation (Lemma 3.2/3.3)

Derivation of the formula. A collector intercepting solar radiation must re‑radiate the unconverted energy. If the collector has an effective radiating area A_{\text{rad}} and operates at temperature T, the radiated power is P_{\text{rad}} = A_{\text{rad}} \sigma T^4 (black‑body approximation). The power that must be dissipated is at least the intercepted power minus the delivered power, but a conservative upper bound is to require that even if the entire intercepted power were converted to heat, it could be radiated without exceeding the material’s maximum temperature T_{\max}. Thus we demand

\eta L_\ast \le A_{\text{rad}} \sigma T_{\max}^4 .

Now, the total collecting area required to intercept \eta L_\ast at a flux \mathcal{F} is A_{\text{collect}} = \eta L_\ast / \mathcal{F}. The ratio of radiating to collecting area is A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}}. Substituting A_{\text{rad}} = (A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}}) A_{\text{collect}} gives

\eta L_\ast \le \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}} \cdot \frac{\eta L_\ast}{\mathcal{F}} \sigma T_{\max}^4 .

Cancelling \eta L_\ast (for \eta>0) and using \mathcal{F} = \sigma T_{\text{eff}}^4 (R_\ast/r)^2 for a collector at distance r from the star. The tightest bound occurs at the closest possible orbit, just above the stellar surface, where \mathcal{F} = \sigma T_{\text{eff}}^4. Then

1 \le \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}} \cdot \frac{\sigma T_{\max}^4}{\sigma T_{\text{eff}}^4} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \left(\frac{T_{\max}}{T_{\text{eff}}}\right)^4 \cdot \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}} \ge 1.

For a fixed design ratio, this translates to an upper bound on \eta; the maximum achievable capture fraction is exactly this value, since if \eta were larger the swarm would overheat even at the closest possible orbit. Hence

\eta_{\max}^{\text{therm}} = \left(\frac{T_{\max}}{T_{\text{eff}}}\right)^4 \cdot \frac{A_{\text{rad}}}{A_{\text{collect}}}.

For the Sun (T_{\text{eff}}=5778 K) and A_{\text{rad}}/A_{\text{collect}}=1, the numerical table in the main text is obtained by evaluating at various T_{\max}.

