Second‑Law Speed Limit – Two‑Regime Form (Theorem 4.3)

C.1 Derivation of the Thermal and Logistics Branches

Definitions. Let one standard collector unit have:

· Collected power: P_0 = \mathcal{F} A_{\text{collect}} (W)
· Mass: m_0 (kg)
· Radiator area: a_{\text{rad},0} (m²)
· Fabrication energy: E_{\text{fab}} = m_0 e_{\text{fab}} (J), where e_{\text{fab}} is specific fabrication energy (J/kg)
· Minimum fabrication time: \tau_{\text{build}} (s)

The swarm consists of N such units, so \eta L_\ast = N P_0 and \dot{\eta} = \dot{N} P_0 / L_\ast.

The total radiator area already deployed is A_{\text{rad}} = N a_{\text{rad},0} = A_{\text{rad,0}} \eta L_\ast, where we define the radiator‑area‑per‑unit‑captured‑power A_{\text{rad,0}} \equiv a_{\text{rad},0}/P_0.

Thermal‑dissipation limit. Fabricating \dot{N} units per second generates waste heat at rate \dot{N} E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}, where \eta_{\text{diss}} \in (0,1] is the fraction of fabrication energy dissipated as heat. This cannot exceed the existing radiator capacity:

\dot{N} E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}} \le A_{\text{rad}} \sigma T_{\max}^4 = A_{\text{rad,0}} \eta L_\ast \sigma T_{\max}^4 .

Hence

\dot{N}_{\text{therm}}(\eta) = \frac{A_{\text{rad,0}} \eta L_\ast \sigma T_{\max}^4}{E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}}.

Converting to \dot{\eta}:

\dot{\eta}_{\text{therm}}(\eta) = \frac{P_0}{L_\ast} \cdot \frac{A_{\text{rad,0}} \eta L_\ast \sigma T_{\max}^4}{E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}} = \frac{P_0 A_{\text{rad,0}} \sigma T_{\max}^4}{E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}} \, \eta .

Using P_0 A_{\text{rad,0}} = P_0 \cdot (a_{\text{rad},0}/P_0) = a_{\text{rad},0} and E_{\text{fab}} = m_0 e_{\text{fab}}, we obtain the specific form

\dot{\eta}_{\text{therm}}(\eta) = \frac{a_{\text{rad},0} \sigma T_{\max}^4}{m_0 e_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}} \eta = \frac{a_{\text{rad,spec}} \sigma T_{\max}^4}{e_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}} \eta \equiv k_{\text{therm}} \eta,

where a_{\text{rad,spec}} = a_{\text{rad},0}/m_0 (m²/kg) is the specific radiator area. Note that L_\ast cancels entirely; the thermal limit depends only on material and thermal properties.

Build‑time limit. No unit can be produced faster than its minimum assembly time:

\dot{N}_{\text{build}} = \frac{1}{\tau_{\text{build}}} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \dot{\eta}_{\text{build}} = \frac{P_0}{L_\ast \tau_{\text{build}}}.

Since both limits are independent necessary conditions, the true maximum is their minimum:

\dot{\eta}_{\max}(\eta) = \min\{k_{\text{therm}} \eta,\; \frac{P_0}{L_\ast \tau_{\text{build}}}\}.

The crossover \eta^\dagger is found by equating the two branches:

k_{\text{therm}} \eta^\dagger = \frac{P_0}{L_\ast \tau_{\text{build}}} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \eta^\dagger = \frac{P_0}{L_\ast k_{\text{therm}} \tau_{\text{build}}}.

Substituting k_{\text{therm}} and P_0 = m_0/m_{\text{specific}} with m_{\text{specific}} = m_0/P_0, we can also write

\eta^\dagger = \frac{E_{\text{fab}} \eta_{\text{diss}}}{\sigma T_{\max}^4 A_{\text{rad,0}} \tau_{\text{build}}},

which matches the form in Theorem 4.3.

C.2 Numerical Estimate of \eta^\dagger (Corollary 4.3)

Using baseline parameters: T_{\max}=2500 K → \sigma T_{\max}^4 = 2.21\times10^6 W/m², a_{\text{rad,spec}} = 1 m²/kg (thin‑film radiator), e_{\text{fab}} = 10^8 J/kg, \eta_{\text{diss}}=1. Then

k_{\text{therm}} = \frac{1 \times 2.21\times10^6}{10^8} \approx 0.022 \text{ s}^{-1}.

For the logistics branch, take a heavy‑lift launch/production capacity \dot{M} = 10^6 kg/yr ≈ 0.0317 kg/s, specific collector mass m_{\text{specific}} \approx 0.0025 kg/W, solar flux \mathcal{F}=1361 W/m². The launch‑limited growth rate coefficient is

k_{\text{build}} = \frac{\dot{M} \mathcal{F}}{m_{\text{specific}} L_\ast} \approx \frac{0.0317 \times 1361}{0.0025 \times 3.828\times10^{26}} \approx 4.5\times10^{-23} \text{ s}^{-1}.

Therefore

\eta^\dagger = \frac{k_{\text{build}}}{k_{\text{therm}}} \approx \frac{4.5\times10^{-23}}{0.022} \approx 2.0\times10^{-21}.

Sensitivity analysis: even if e_{\text{fab}} is increased to 10^{10} J/kg (100× baseline) and a_{\text{rad,spec}} reduced to 0.01 m²/kg (100× worse), k_{\text{therm}} becomes 2.2\times10^{-4} s⁻¹, and \eta^\dagger \approx 2\times10^{-19}, still well below \eta_c. Thus the thermal branch is never binding for practical buildout.

