Appendix D

Automation Dynamics and Critical Threshold

D.1 Logistic Learning Curves for Sub‑Autonomies

The differential equation

\frac{d\alpha_i}{dt} = \kappa_i \alpha_i(1-\alpha_i) I_{\text{robotics}}, \qquad i = \text{mining, manuf, assembly},

has the standard sigmoidal solution. If I_{\text{robotics}} is approximately constant over the timescale of interest, separation of variables yields

\alpha_i(t) = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1-\alpha_i(0)}{\alpha_i(0)} e^{-\kappa_i I_{\text{robotics}} t}} .

The overall autonomy is \alpha = \min_i \alpha_i, which thus evolves as a bottleneck determined by the slowest‑learning component.

D.2 Lemma 5.3 – Critical Threshold for Exponential Runaway

Statement. At early times when material and thermal limits are negligible, the growth rate is \dot{\eta} = (\epsilon \zeta \delta \alpha \mu - \lambda_d) \eta. The system can sustain exponential growth without external (human) inputs only if \epsilon \zeta \delta \alpha \mu > \lambda_d. Solving for \alpha gives the critical autonomy

\alpha_c = \frac{\lambda_d}{\epsilon \zeta \delta \mu}.

If \alpha < \alpha_c, the infrastructure decays without continuous external supply; once \alpha exceeds \alpha_c, the loop becomes self‑amplifying.

Proof. The condition \dot{\eta} > 0 for \eta>0 is \epsilon \zeta \delta \alpha \mu > \lambda_d. Given the other parameters, the smallest \alpha that satisfies this is \alpha_c. Because \zeta,\delta,\mu are themselves state‑dependent and generally increase over time, \alpha_c decreases as the system matures, making it progressively easier to cross the threshold. ∎

