Appendix F

Resilience ODE – Derivation from a Shock Model

F.1 Stochastic Shock Process

Assume failure events occur according to a Poisson process with rate \Lambda. Each shock independently destroys a random fraction X of the total capacity, where X \in [0,1] has probability density f_X(x) and mean \mathbb{E}[X]. Between shocks, the system repairs at a rate proportional to the capacity shortfall and the investment in resilience. Let the nominal capacity be C_{\text{nom}}; the actual capacity is C = \rho C_{\text{nom}}.

A mean‑field deterministic approximation (valid when shocks are small and repairs are fast relative to the growth of \eta) yields

\frac{d\rho}{dt} = \nu I_{\text{redundancy}} (1-\rho) - \Lambda \mathbb{E}[X] \rho .

Derivation. In an infinitesimal time dt, a shock occurs with probability \Lambda dt and causes a fractional loss of capacity X; the expected capacity after the shock is (1 - \mathbb{E}[X])\rho C_{\text{nom}}. The repair process adds capacity at a rate proportional to the shortfall: \nu I_{\text{redundancy}} (C_{\text{nom}} - C) = \nu I_{\text{redundancy}} (1-\rho)C_{\text{nom}}. Taking the limit dt \to 0 and dividing by C_{\text{nom}} gives the ODE. The shock term is an average; a rigorous stochastic differential equation would involve a jump process, but for the macrostate \rho the mean dynamics are sufficient for CETT. ∎

Steady‑state resilience: Setting \dot\rho=0 yields \rho_{\text{ss}} = \frac{\nu I_{\text{redundancy}}}{\nu I_{\text{redundancy}} + \Lambda \mathbb{E}[X]}, which is less than 1 unless there is no shock risk.

