Appendix H

Optimal Control Analysis

H.1 Full Hamiltonian and Costate Equations

We consider the simplified system (8.1)–(8.2) with control \mathbf{u}=(\epsilon, u_\alpha)\in[0,1]^2. The current‑value Hamiltonian is

\mathcal{H} = U(\eta) + V(\alpha) + \lambda_\eta \big[ (1-u_\alpha)\epsilon \zeta_0 \delta \alpha \mu \eta - \lambda_d \eta \big] + \lambda_\alpha \big[ \kappa_\alpha u_\alpha \epsilon \eta L_\ast \delta \alpha(1-\alpha) \big].

The costate equations are

\begin{aligned}
\dot\lambda_\eta &= r\lambda_\eta - \frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\eta}, \\
\dot\lambda_\alpha &= r\lambda_\alpha - \frac{\partial\mathcal{H}}{\partial\alpha},
\end{aligned}

with transversality conditions \lim_{t\to\infty} e^{-rt}\lambda_\eta\eta = 0 and similarly for \alpha.

H.2 Switching Functions and Bang‑Bang Property

The Hamiltonian is linear in \epsilon:

\mathcal{H} = (\text{terms without }\epsilon) + \epsilon \Big[ \lambda_\eta (1-u_\alpha)\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu\eta + \lambda_\alpha \kappa_\alpha u_\alpha \eta L_\ast \delta \alpha(1-\alpha) \Big].

Define the switching function \Phi_\epsilon(t) \equiv \lambda_\eta (1-u_\alpha)\zeta_0\delta\alpha\mu\eta + \lambda_\alpha \kappa_\alpha u_\alpha \eta L_\ast \delta \alpha(1-\alpha). The maximum principle requires \epsilon^*(t) = 1 if \Phi_\epsilon(t) > 0, \epsilon^*(t) = 0 if \Phi_\epsilon(t) < 0. For u_\alpha, the Hamiltonian is linear once \epsilon>0:

\mathcal{H} = (\text{terms without }u_\alpha) + \epsilon \eta \delta \alpha u_\alpha \big[ -\lambda_\eta \zeta_0 \mu + \lambda_\alpha \kappa_\alpha L_\ast (1-\alpha) \big].

Hence the switching function is \Phi_{u_\alpha} = \epsilon \eta \delta \alpha \big[ -\lambda_\eta \zeta_0 \mu + \lambda_\alpha \kappa_\alpha L_\ast (1-\alpha) \big]. The optimal u_\alpha^* is 1 if \Phi_{u_\alpha}>0 and 0 if \Phi_{u_\alpha}<0.

H.3 Singular Arc Analysis (Sketch)

A singular arc occurs if \Phi_\epsilon(t) \equiv 0 on an interval. Then all time derivatives must also vanish. From \dot\Phi_\epsilon = 0 we obtain an algebraic relation among the costates and states; from \ddot\Phi_\epsilon = 0 we obtain another. Together with the costate dynamics and the Hamiltonian constancy (if autonomous), these conditions can be analysed. Under strict concavity of U and V and a positive discount rate, the singular arc conditions typically lead to a contradiction except at isolated points; thus the optimal control is bang‑bang almost everywhere. A complete rigorous proof is lengthy and will be presented elsewhere.

