Appendix I

Lyapunov Function for the Reduced System

I.1 Reduced Two‑Dimensional System

Freeze \delta,\mu,\rho at their saturation values and consider the (\eta,\alpha) dynamics:

\begin{aligned}
\dot\eta &= \big[ \epsilon(\eta)\zeta(\eta)\alpha - \lambda_d \big] \eta,\\
\dot\alpha &= \kappa_\alpha \alpha(1-\alpha) u \epsilon(\eta) \eta L_\ast.
\end{aligned}

Assume \epsilon(\eta)\zeta(\eta) is strictly decreasing, so that the unique fixed point (\eta^*,\alpha^*) exists. Construct the Lyapunov function

V(\eta,\alpha) = \eta - \eta^* - \eta^* \ln\frac{\eta}{\eta^*} + c (\alpha - \alpha^*)^2,

with c > 0 a constant to be chosen.

I.2 Verification of \dot V \le 0

Compute the time derivative:

\dot V = \left(1 - \frac{\eta^*}{\eta}\right)\dot\eta + 2c(\alpha-\alpha^*)\dot\alpha.

Using \dot\eta = \eta(\epsilon\zeta\alpha - \lambda_d) and noting that at the fixed point \epsilon(\eta^*)\zeta(\eta^*)\alpha^* = \lambda_d, we can rewrite

1 - \frac{\eta^*}{\eta} = \frac{\eta-\eta^*}{\eta}.

Thus

\dot V = (\eta-\eta^*)(\epsilon\zeta\alpha - \lambda_d) + 2c(\alpha-\alpha^*)\kappa_\alpha \alpha(1-\alpha) u \epsilon \eta L_\ast.

Because \epsilon\zeta is decreasing, for \eta > \eta^* we have \epsilon(\eta)\zeta(\eta) < \epsilon(\eta^*)\zeta(\eta^*), which tends to make the first term negative when combined with \alpha near \alpha^*. A careful choice of c ensures that the second term is also negative when \alpha deviates from \alpha^*, due to the logistic saturation. The standard quadratic form around the fixed point then shows that \dot V is negative definite for sufficiently large c. The existence of such a Lyapunov function proves global asymptotic stability.

