Appendix J

Bifurcation Under Non‑Monotonic Reinvestment

J.1 Formulation

Relax the assumption that \epsilon(\eta) is non‑increasing. Consider a parametric form

\epsilon(\eta) = \epsilon_0 + \epsilon_1 \eta e^{-\beta\eta},

with \epsilon_0,\epsilon_1,\beta>0. This function increases for small \eta (if \epsilon_1 is sufficiently large) and decays to \epsilon_0 as \eta\to\infty. The net growth rate r(\eta) = \epsilon(\eta)\zeta(\eta)\alpha\mu - \lambda_d can then have two positive roots \eta_1^*<\eta_2^* if the hump is high enough.

J.2 Phase‑Line Analysis

The scalar ODE \dot\eta = r(\eta)\eta has fixed points at \eta=0 and at the roots of r(\eta). Since r(\eta) is positive for \eta between the roots and negative outside, \eta_1^* is an unstable fixed point (source) and \eta_2^* is stable. The origin \eta=0 is also a fixed point; its stability depends on the sign of r(0). If r(0) < 0 (e.g., because \epsilon_0\zeta_0\alpha\mu < \lambda_d), then \eta=0 is stable, and the system exhibits bistability: trajectories starting below \eta_1^* collapse to zero; trajectories starting above \eta_1^* are attracted to the high‑capacity equilibrium \eta_2^*.

This threshold behaviour formalises the idea of an “ignition” phase, where a temporary surge in investment can push the system past the unstable equilibrium and into a self‑sustaining growth regime.


